# <Typology> — area-measurement convention

This is the decision rule 71 step 3 requires be made explicitly BEFORE the first `define_room`
call. Getting this wrong is exactly what caused the hospital build's systematic 15-25% area
mismatch, discovered only after ~90 rooms already existed with the wrong convention baked into
their boundary polygons.

## Decision

- **Convention:** <net internal (to face-of-finish) | gross (to structural axis)> — pick one.
- **Citation:** <e.g. PN-ISO 9836 for Polish net/gross definitions, or the typology's own code if
  it defines area differently for compliance purposes (e.g. a minimum-area rule that explicitly
  means net clear floor area, not gross)>.

## Practical consequence for `define_room`

- Wall thickness in this typology's typical construction: **<X> mm** (informs the inset).
- `define_room`'s boundary polygon vertices must be drawn **<inset by half the wall thickness
  from the wall centreline | on the wall centreline, matching a gross convention>** — state which,
  explicitly, so it's not rediscovered by comparing a label to a flood-fill measurement after
  the fact.
- Any validator rule checking a minimum area for this typology (`area_at_least` in
  `validators/_standards/<typology>-baseline.yaml`) is checking the BOUNDARY POLYGON's own area,
  not the flood-fill measurement — so if the polygon isn't drawn to this convention, the
  validator rule silently checks the wrong number even after `correct_room_area(syncBoundary=true)`
  fixes the label text. Get the polygon right at construction time.

## Sourcing note

State whether this typology's minimum-area citations (in `STANDARDS.md`) were written assuming
net or gross area — if the source document doesn't say, that's itself worth recording as a gap.
